John H. wrote:
> Back in the Bad Old Days before the UbiquiNet, much free software was
> passed around by hand, often at user group meetings.  It was there
> that Amiga owners came to bless the name of Fred Fish, who accumulated
> a library of over a thousand floppy disks and distributed copies to
> user groups, who would then give copies to members for the cost of
> media.  It turns out that Fred Fish died, maybe unexpectedly, on April
> 20.
> 
> Back then, this is where many Amiga users would get their copies of
> Hack, Larn, Omega, HackLite or Nethack.
> 
> The Fred Fish archives can be found at http://ftp.funet.fi/pub/amiga/fish/
> An exhaustive listing of their contents is at http://www.amiga-stuff.com/pd/fish.html
> 
> For those with a running Amiga or an emulator, these are the disks on
> which roguelikes can be found in the archive:
> 7, 25, 62: Hack
> 63: Larn
> 187, 799: HackLite (an Amiga-specific port)
> 320, 528: AmiOmega
> 189-190, 460, 822: Nethack (822 contains 3.1, which is relatively
> close to what we have today).
> 
> If source is available, it's either in the archive or in a subsequent
> disk.

What a shame, I remember the good old Fred Fish archives... I wasn't 
aware of the funet archives. I'll get the relevant files and add them to 
my archive:

